\section*{Introduction}
Graphs are prevalent across numerous domains, such as social networks, knowledge graphs, and transportation networks. Consequently, graph embedding has become a critical area of research, with applications spanning graph classification, node classification, link prediction, and graph clustering. This paper focuses on graph retrieval, a task that involves identifying the most similar nodes to a query node within a graph. This capability is essential for many practical applications, including recommendation systems, recommendation ranking, and recommendation search.

Existing graph embedding methods typically rely on random walks or matrix factorization to generate node embeddings. While these approaches are effective for tasks like classification and clustering, they often fail to scale efficiently when applied to large-scale graphs. To overcome this limitation, we propose a new graph embedding method that incorporates a hierarchical structure to improve retrieval efficiency. Our approach is designed to be scalable for large graphs and can be readily adapted for other tasks.

\section*{Contributions}
Our primary contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We introduce a new graph embedding method that employs a hierarchical structure to represent both local and global graph properties.
\item We develop a novel graph retrieval algorithm that utilizes the hierarchical structure of our embeddings to achieve faster performance.
\item We demonstrate that our method surpasses state-of-the-art techniques in terms of efficiency.
\item We show that our approach is highly effective for various downstream tasks, including graph classification, node classification, and link prediction.
\end{itemize}

