\section*{Introduction}
Task-oriented dialogue (ToD) involves interactions where users and agents exchange information to accomplish specific objectives. ToD datasets typically include user utterances, system responses, and dialogue state information. A common approach to building ToD systems is to first train a dialogue state tracker (DST) to identify the user's intent and current state, followed by a response generator that produces the next system utterance. However, DSTs are typically trained on a single domain, and extending them to multiple domains is challenging.

This paper presents a new multi-domain ToD dataset and a corresponding model. Our dataset consists of 1,000 multi-domain dialogues, with each conversation averaging 10.5 turns. We developed a DST that employs a single encoder to capture shared semantic information across different domains. This approach reduces the number of parameters by 50% compared to previous methods while maintaining comparable performance. Furthermore, we demonstrate that our model achieves state-of-the-art results on both single-domain and multi-domain ToD benchmarks.

