\section*{Introduction}
Reinforcement learning (RL) is a machine learning paradigm where an agent learns to make decisions by interacting with an environment to maximize cumulative rewards. RL has achieved remarkable success in diverse domains, including autonomous vehicles, robotics, and video games. Despite these achievements, RL remains notoriously difficult to master, often demanding substantial computational power and careful hyperparameter tuning. This paper presents a novel RL algorithm that enhances the performance of other methods by introducing a new reward function based on the distance to the nearest previously visited state. Our results indicate that this approach accelerates learning and improves overall performance. Additionally, we demonstrate that our algorithm can be combined with other RL techniques to achieve even better outcomes. We have tested our method on various RL tasks, including the CartPole and MountainCar environments, and it has proven effective across a broad spectrum of RL applications. The code can be accessed at https://github.com/abhishek1999/RL.

\section*{Related Work}
Reinforcement learning has been a focal point of research for decades, with numerous algorithms developed to address diverse problems. These include Q-learning, Deep Q-Networks (DQN), Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), and others. These algorithms have successfully tackled complex challenges such as self-driving cars, robotics, and video games. However, they often require significant computational resources and tuning to achieve optimal performance. Our work builds upon these existing methods by introducing a novel reward function that leverages the distance to the nearest previously visited state. This modification allows our algorithm to outperform traditional approaches on various RL tasks.

